H('3. Results')
H('3.1. A single clade contains chromosomal EptA and all mobilised families',3)
RICH([('The 161 proteins resolved into three groups that correspond to the three ',False),('E. coli',True),(' paralogues (Figure 1). The clade anchored by EptA contained 76 sequences, of which 65 were chromosomal and 11 were mobilised. The clade anchored by EptB contained 36 sequences and the clade anchored by CptA/EptC contained 49. No sequence fell outside these three groups. Chromosomal members of the EptA clade came from 44 genus-level lineages, among them ',False),('Escherichia',True),(', ',False),('Salmonella',True),(', ',False),('Klebsiella',True),(', ',False),('Citrobacter',True),(', ',False),('Enterobacter',True),(', ',False),('Serratia',True),(', ',False),('Yersinia',True),(', ',False),('Pectobacterium',True),(' and ',False),('Dickeya',True),(', together with ',False),('Aeromonas',True),(', ',False),('Shewanella',True),(', ',False),('Vibrio',True),(', ',False),('Plesiomonas',True),(', ',False),('Moraxella',True),(', ',False),('Cupriavidus',True),(', ',False),('Pseudomonas',True),(', ',False),('Burkholderia',True),(', ',False),('Neisseria',True),(' and ',False),('Campylobacter',True),('.',False)])
RICH([('Within that clade the mobilised proteins were dispersed rather than clustered. The smallest group containing all 11 of them also contained 63 chromosomal proteins and received only 28% bootstrap support, which is what would be expected if the families entered the mobile gene pool independently. Several small groupings were, by contrast, strongly supported. MCR-1.1, MCR-2.1 and MCR-6.1 formed a group with the ',False),('Faucicola osloensis',True),(' protein WP_490288700.1 and the ',False),('Moraxellaceae',True),(' protein WP_284034866.1 at 100% bootstrap support. MCR-9.1 grouped with the ',False),('Citrobacter freundii',True),(' protein WP_261396260.1 at 100% support, and MCR-10.1 joined that pair together with ',False),('Buttiauxella cochleicola',True),(' WP_232921000.1. MCR-5.1 grouped with two ',False),('Cupriavidus',True),(' proteins at 100% support. MCR-3.1 and MCR-7.1 fell among the ',False),('Aeromonas',True),(', ',False),('Buttiauxella',True),(' and ',False),('Citrobacter',True),(' sequences, and MCR-4.1 grouped with ',False),('Shewanella',True),('.',False)])
H('3.2. Each family has a distinct closest chromosomal relative',3)
RICH([('Identity to the nearest chromosomal protein varied more than twofold across the families (Figure 2b). MCR-9.1 was 99.0% identical to WP_261396260.1 of ',False),('C. freundii',True),(', a 400-residue partial protein that is most plausibly an ',False),('mcr-9',True),('-like gene resident in that genome. MCR-10.1 was 84.6% identical to WP_232921000.1 of ',False),('B. cochleicola',True),(', MCR-4.1 was 80.2% identical to WP_220053229.1 of ',False),('Shewanella',True),(' sp., and MCR-7.1 was 78.3% identical to WP_499669208.1 of ',False),('Aeromonas jandaei',True),('. The three Moraxellaceae-associated families sat lower, at 62.4% for MCR-1.1, 64.1% for MCR-2.1 and 65.1% for MCR-6.1 against ',False),('F. osloensis',True),(' WP_490288700.1. MCR-3.1 reached 66.9% against the ',False),('C. freundii',True),(' protein and 65.9% against ',False),('B. cochleicola',True),('. At the other end of the range, MCR-8.1 reached only 47.9%, MCR-5.1 45.1% against ',False),('Cupriavidus',True),(' and MCR-12.1 41.0% against a ',False),('Pseudomonas',True),(' protein.',False)])
RICH([('The families were, in most cases, more distant from each other than from their chromosomal relatives. Pairwise identity between reference alleles ranged from 31.1% to 87.9%, with a mean of 41.7% (Figure 2a). Three blocks stood out. MCR-1, MCR-2 and MCR-6 shared 81.4% to 87.9% identity; MCR-3 and MCR-7 shared 69.8%; MCR-9 and MCR-10 shared 82.9%. Across the blocks, values fell to between 31% and 46%. Identity to ',False),('E. coli',True),(' EptA ranged from 33.1% for MCR-1.1 to 41.8% for MCR-8.1, so no family is a recent derivative of the ',False),('E. coli',True),(' chromosomal gene.',False)])
H('3.3. The catalytic apparatus is invariant and older than mobilisation',3)
P('MCR-1.1 has a hydrophobic amino-terminal region that ends near residue 183 and a periplasmic domain of about 358 residues (Figure 3a, 3b). Across the whole dataset the predicted membrane-spanning segments numbered three to five and the periplasmic domain occupied between 357 and 382 residues in the mobilised proteins, so the two-domain architecture is uniform.',indent=False)
P('Sixteen positions reported from the MCR-1 structures were examined (Table 1). Glu246, Thr285, His390, His395, Asp465 and His466 were occupied by the same residue in all 11 mobilised proteins and in all 150 chromosomal proteins. Lys333 and His478 were invariant in the mobilised set and in the chromosomal members of the EptA clade but fell to 45% and 69% occupancy across the full chromosomal set, which includes the EptB and CptA/EptC paralogues. The six cysteines that form the three disulfide bonds behaved in the same way. Cys281, Cys291, Cys356, Cys364, Cys414 and Cys422 were present in every mobilised protein and in every chromosomal member of the EptA clade, and dropped to between 43% and 67% elsewhere. Conservation profiles (Figure 3c) show the pattern directly. The periplasmic domain is more conserved than the membrane anchor in all four groups, and the peaks coincide with the catalytic positions.',indent=False)
H('3.4. Ancestral reconstruction places the catalytic set before mobilisation',3)
P('Marginal reconstruction recovered the full catalytic set at every node subtending a mobilised family. At the node joining MCR-1, MCR-2 and MCR-6 to the Moraxellaceae proteins, Glu246, Cys281, Thr285, Cys291, Lys333, Cys356, Cys364, His390, His395, Cys414, Cys422, Asp465, His466 and His478 were all reconstructed with posterior probabilities of 1.00. The same was true at the nodes subtending MCR-3 with MCR-7, MCR-9 with MCR-10, MCR-4 with Shewanella and MCR-5 with Cupriavidus, and at the ancestor of the whole EptA clade. Mean marginal posterior probability across 808 alignment columns ranged from 0.61 at the Cupriavidus node to 0.74 at the mcr-9 and mcr-10 node. MCR-1.1 differs from the reconstructed pre-mobilisation ancestor at 109 of its 541 positions, a divergence of 20.1%, none of which falls at a catalytic residue.',indent=False)
H('3.5. Purifying selection and heterogeneous base composition',3)
RICH([('Codon-based comparisons between families gave dN/dS values of 0.081 to 0.351, with a median of 0.224 across the 12 pairs for which the estimator converged (Figure 5a). Comparisons between each mobilised gene and its chromosomal progenitor gave a similar range. Values were 0.164 for ',False),('mcr-2',True),(' and 0.100 for ',False),('mcr-6',True),(' against the ',False),('F. osloensis',True),(' gene, 0.114, 0.159 and 0.126 for ',False),('mcr-1',True),(', ',False),('mcr-2',True),(' and ',False),('mcr-6',True),(' against the ',False),('Moraxella',True),(' sp. gene, and 0.324 for ',False),('mcr-12',True),(' against the ',False),('Pseudomonas',True),(' gene (Figure 5b). The comparisons for ',False),('mcr-1',True),(' against ',False),('F. osloensis',True),(' and for ',False),('mcr-4',True),(' against ',False),('Shewanella',True),(' were saturated at synonymous sites and are not reported as ratios. Purifying selection is therefore the dominant regime both within the mobilised pool and between the mobilised genes and their chromosomal counterparts.',False)])
RICH([('Base composition was markedly heterogeneous (Figure 5c). G+C content of the reference alleles ranged from 40.1% for ',False),('mcr-4',True),(' to 58.3% for ',False),('mcr-12',True),(', and third-position G+C from 37.5% for ',False),('mcr-8',True),(' to 76.9% for ',False),('mcr-7',True),('. Several families sit well away from the value of roughly 50% typical of ',False),('E. coli',True),(' and ',False),('Klebsiella',True),(' chromosomes. The donor loci were themselves compositionally distinct, at 45.1% for ',False),('F. osloensis',True),(', 46.1% for ',False),('Moraxella',True),(' sp., 46.4% for ',False),('Shewanella',True),(' sp. and 57.7% for ',False),('Pseudomonas',True),(' sp.',False)])
H('3.6. Mobile elements flank the mobilised copies but not the chromosomal genes',3)
RICH([('Six loci carried annotation informative enough to describe the neighbourhood of the transferase gene (Figure 4). On pHNSHP45, ',False),('mcr-1',True),(' lies with an ISApl1 transposase inside a 3.7 kb element annotated as a composite transposon, downstream of the ',False),('nikAB',True),(' mobilisation genes. On pKP37-BE, ',False),('mcr-2',True),(' is flanked on both sides by proteins annotated as mobile element proteins. On pMCR4.3 a ',False),('tnpA',True),(' gene lies immediately downstream of ',False),('mcr-4',True),('. The ',False),('mcr-5',True),(' deposit carries ',False),('tnpR',True),(' and a 3.0 kb Tn3-family ',False),('tnpA',True),(' downstream of the transferase and an IS5/IS1182 transposase at the far end of the contig. On pKP91, ',False),('mcr-8',True),(' lies 4 kb from a DDE-family transposase. The ',False),('mcr-7.1',True),(' deposit was the exception, with mobilisation genes ',False),('mobC',True),(' and ',False),('mbeA',True),(' present but no transposase within 8 kb.',False)])
RICH([('The chromosomal picture was different. In ',False),('F. osloensis',True),(' the transferase gene lies between a PAP2-family lipid A phosphatase and an Rhs-family protein, within a stretch of housekeeping genes that includes ',False),('glnD',True),(', ',False),('hemA',True),(', ',False),('lolB',True),(' and ',False),('tyrS',True),('. Across the four donor loci examined, 40 predicted open reading frames within 9 kb of the transferase gene returned no significant match to any of 266 transposase profiles. Two neighbours are worth noting. The ',False),('mcr-7.1',True),(' plasmid carries ',False),('dgkA',True),(' and a PAP2-family gene immediately downstream of the transferase, and ',False),('mcr-8',True),(' is followed by ',False),('copR',True),(', ',False),('baeS',True),(' and ',False),('dgkA',True),('. Both arrangements echo chromosomal neighbourhoods and suggest that mobilisation captured flanking host sequence along with the transferase gene.',False)])
H('3.7. Candidate chromosomal reservoirs',3)
RICH([('Ranking the 150 chromosomal proteins by their identity to the closest mobilised family gives a short list of plausible sources for further mobilisation (Supplementary Table S1). Eight proteins reached at least 60% identity and five reached at least 70%. The top of the list is occupied by the taxa already implicated, namely ',False),('C. freundii',True),(' at 99.0%, ',False),('B. cochleicola',True),(' at 87.4%, ',False),('Shewanella',True),(' sp. at 80.2% and three ',False),('Aeromonas',True),(' species between 67.1% and 78.3%, followed by ',False),('F. osloensis',True),(' at 65.1% and a second Moraxellaceae protein at 64.5%. Below that threshold sits a broad second tier between 50% and 57% that has not so far been associated with mobilisation, comprising ',False),('Edwardsiella tarda',True),(', ',False),('Hafnia alvei',True),(', ',False),('Ewingella',True),(' sp., ',False),('Rahnella perminowiae',True),(', ',False),('Plesiomonas shigelloides',True),(' and four ',False),('Vibrio',True),(' species including ',False),('V. cholerae',True),('. The median chromosomal member of the EptA clade shares 42.8% identity with its closest mobilised family, so the second tier is well above the background of the clade.',False)])

# ---------------- Discussion ----------------
H('4. Discussion')
RICH([('The ten ',False),('mcr',True),(' families do not share a single origin. They occupy one clade, the clade that also contains chromosomal EptA, but within it they are scattered among chromosomal sequences from taxa that are not closely related to one another. Moraxellaceae, ',False),('Shewanella',True),(', ',False),('Aeromonas',True),(', ',False),('Cupriavidus',True),(', ',False),('Buttiauxella',True),(' and ',False),('Citrobacter',True),(' each sit closest to a different family. The simplest reading is that at least seven separate capture events moved a chromosomal gene onto a mobile element, and that the genes so captured were already competent phosphoethanolamine transferases when they left.',False)])
P('The invariance of the catalytic set supports that reading directly. Glu246, Thr285, the histidine pair at 390 and 395, Asp465 and His466 are occupied identically in every protein examined, mobilised or not, and ancestral reconstruction recovers all of them at every node subtending a mobilised family with maximum posterior probability. Nothing in the data suggests that the transition from chromosome to plasmid involved a change in the active site. The three disulfide bonds tell a slightly different story. They are invariant within the EptA clade and only sporadically present in the EptB and CptA/EptC paralogues, which marks them as a feature of this particular lineage rather than of the superfamily.',indent=False)
RICH([('Where the transition does show is in the flanking DNA. Five of the six informative deposits place a transposase or an insertion sequence within a few kilobases of the mobilised gene, the ISApl1 arrangement around ',False),('mcr-1',True),(' being the clearest case and consistent with the transposition model proposed for Tn6330 (Snesrud et al., 2016, 2018; Poirel et al., 2017). The chromosomal donor loci show no such elements. Four regions of 18 kb each, screened against 266 transposase profiles, returned nothing. The chromosomal genes sit among housekeeping functions, which is where one would expect a gene that has been part of the core lipid A modification machinery for a long time. Mobility is a property of the context, not of the gene.',False)])
RICH([('The identity values distribute in a way that is informative about timing. MCR-9 and its ',False),('C. freundii',True),(' counterpart differ by a single per cent, and MCR-10 and MCR-4 are within 15 to 20 per cent of their nearest chromosomal relatives. Those events look recent. The Moraxellaceae-associated families sit at 62% to 65%, which is close enough to identify the donor lineage with confidence but far enough to indicate that the sampled chromosomal genes are relatives of the true progenitor rather than the progenitor itself. MCR-5, MCR-8 and MCR-12, at 41% to 48%, are either older or drawn from lineages that remain unsampled. The compositional data point the same way. Third-position G+C ranges from 37.5% to 76.9% across the families, which is a wide spread for genes now resident in hosts with genomes near 50% G+C, and it indicates that amelioration after transfer has been incomplete.',False)])
RICH([('The practical implication concerns surveillance. Genomic surveillance for colistin resistance is built around the ten named families, and a gene that is 50% identical to any of them would not be flagged by the reference-based tools in routine use. The second tier identified here, ',False),('Edwardsiella',True),(', ',False),('Hafnia',True),(', ',False),('Ewingella',True),(', ',False),('Rahnella',True),(', ',False),('Plesiomonas',True),(' and ',False),('Vibrio',True),(', contains organisms that share aquatic and food-production environments with the taxa that have already contributed. ',False),('Aeromonas',True),(' and ',False),('Shewanella',True),(' were once in the same position, unremarkable environmental genera carrying a chromosomal gene of no clinical interest, until two of their genes appeared on plasmids in ',False),('Salmonella',True),(' and ',False),('E. coli',True),('. Surveillance that searches for the enzyme family rather than for the ten known alleles would detect the next event earlier.',False)])
P('Several limitations apply. Sampling was one representative per cluster per genus, which captures the breadth of the family but not the within-species diversity that would refine a donor assignment to the level of a strain. The deep nodes of the tree received low bootstrap support, as is usual for a gene family with this much divergence, so the conclusion that mobilisation happened repeatedly rests on the well-supported shallow groupings and on the taxonomic identity of the nearest relatives, not on the deep topology. Synonymous divergence was saturated for two of the progenitor comparisons, which limits what the selection analysis can say about the earliest stages after transfer. The Citrobacter protein that anchors the mcr-9 grouping is a partial sequence of 400 residues and is best regarded as an mcr-9-like gene already present in that genome rather than as an untouched chromosomal progenitor. Finally, the analysis is a comparison of sequences; the enzymatic activity of the candidate reservoir genes, and the level of resistance they would confer if expressed from a plasmid, remain to be tested.',indent=False)

H('5. Conclusions')
RICH([('Every named ',False),('mcr',True),(' family belongs to the chromosomal EptA lineage, and each family entered the mobile gene pool from a different part of it. The catalytic machinery was complete before mobilisation and has been maintained under purifying selection since. What changed was the genomic context, and the chromosomal genes that now sit closest to the mobilised families are found in genera that share environments with food animals and water. Those genes, and the wider EptA clade in ',False),('Aeromonas',True),(', ',False),('Shewanella',True),(', ',False),('Buttiauxella',True),(', ',False),('Vibrio',True),(' and the Moraxellaceae, are where the next mobilised colistin resistance determinant is most likely to originate.',False)])

